    <p>Rural shops, pole barns, and detached garages mean this county runs more oversized and second doors than a typical suburb — and those bigger doors are exactly where the hardware has to be sized right the first time.</p>

    <h2>When It's a Same-Day Call</h2>

    <p>A broken spring is a today problem — the car's trapped or the shop's standing open — and most Cullman companies run same-day spring service for exactly that reason. If a door still works but is getting loud, jerky, or slow, that's worn rollers and hinges talking, and servicing them before a cable frays is cheaper than the failure it prevents. If a spring's already broken once on a decade-old door, ask about replacing both springs and the worn cables together rather than one part at a time.</p>

    <h2>Garage Door Questions Cullman Owners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Opener hums but the door won't move — new opener?</strong></p>
    <p>Usually not. It's almost always a snapped torsion spring; the opener can't lift the door's full weight without it. Look for a 2-inch gap in the spring coil.</p>

    <p><strong>Can I change the spring myself?</strong></p>
    <p>No — especially on a wide shop door. Torsion springs hold enough tension to seriously injure you. This is the one garage job that genuinely needs a pro.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Spring replacement $150 to $350 same day, a new opener $350 to $600, a full door $900 to $2,500.</p>

